{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ZEcZe65jdKvhMTPYuqJKg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd087-sizeparse\n2\ttitle: \"pkg/sizeparse: Size String Parser with Unit Suffixes\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Eight cmd/ utilities (head, tail, split, truncate, numfmt, du, sort, od) need to parse\n6\t  size strings with unit suffixes (K, M, G, T for powers of 1024; KB, MB, GB for powers\n7\t  of 1000; b for 512). In run 37, each utility independently implemented suffix parsing\n8\t  with slight variations, totaling ~50 lines per utility. This duplication risks\n9\t  inconsistent suffix handling across utilities.\n10\t\n11\t  pkg/sizeparse provides a single parser that handles all GNU coreutils suffix\n12\t  conventions. Each cmd/ utility imports this package instead of reimplementing suffix\n13\t  multiplication.\n14\t\n15\tgoals:\n16\t  - G1: Parse size strings with binary (IEC) and decimal (SI) unit suffixes.\n17\t  - G2: Support the full GNU coreutils suffix set including b, K, KiB, KB, M, MiB, MB, G, GiB, GB, T, TiB, TB, P, PiB, PB, E, EiB, EB, Z, ZiB, ZB, Y, YiB, YB.\n18\t  - G3: Support optional sign prefixes (+, -) for relative size adjustments.\n19\t\n20\trequirements:\n21\t  R1:\n22\t    title: Size parsing\n23\t    items:\n24\t      - R1.1:\n25\t          text: |\n26\t            Must provide func Parse(s string) (int64, error) that parses a size string\n27\t            consisting of an optional sign (+ or -), a decimal integer, and an optional\n28\t            unit suffix. Returns the size in bytes. Returns an error for invalid input.\n29\t      - R1.2:\n30\t          text: |\n31\t            Must support binary (IEC) suffixes: K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1024^2),\n32\t            G or GiB (1024^3), T or TiB (1024^4), P or PiB (1024^5), E or EiB (1024^6),\n33\t            Z or ZiB (1024^7), Y or YiB (1024^8). Must also support b (512) for\n34\t            POSIX compatibility.\n35\t      - R1.3:\n36\t          text: |\n37\t            Must support decimal (SI) suffixes: KB (1000), MB (1000^2), GB (1000^3),\n38\t            TB (1000^4), PB (1000^5), EB (1000^6), ZB (1000^7), YB (1000^8).\n39\t      - R1.4:\n40\t          text: Must preserve the sign in the returned value. \"+100K\" returns +102400, \"-50M\" returns -52428800. No sign prefix means positive.\n41\t\n42\t  R2:\n43\t    title: Extended parsing options\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R2.1:\n46\t          text: |\n47\t            Must provide func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\n48\t            where ParseOptions allows configuring behavior:\n49\t\n50\t              type ParseOptions struct {\n51\t                  AllowSign    bool // allow +/- prefix (default false for Parse)\n52\t                  DefaultUnit  int64 // multiplier when no suffix given (default 1)\n53\t              }\n54\t      - R2.2:\n55\t          text: \"ParseWithOptions with DefaultUnit=1024 and input '5' returns 5120 (5 * 1024). This supports utilities like du where the default unit is blocks.\"\n56\t\n57\t  R3:\n58\t    title: Constraints\n59\t    items:\n60\t      - R3.1:\n61\t          text: Must return an error when the numeric part overflows int64.\n62\t      - R3.2:\n63\t          text: Must return an error when the suffix is not recognized.\n64\t\n65\tnon_goals:\n66\t  - pkg/sizeparse does not format sizes for output; use pkg/format.HumanSize for that.\n67\t  - pkg/sizeparse does not handle floating-point sizes.\n68\t\n69\tpackage_contract:\n70\t  exports:\n71\t    - name: ParseOptions\n72\t      signature: \"type ParseOptions struct { AllowSign bool; DefaultUnit int64 }\"\n73\t    - name: Parse\n74\t      signature: \"func Parse(s string) (int64, error)\"\n75\t    - name: ParseWithOptions\n76\t      signature: \"func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\"\n77\t  imports:\n78\t    - fmt\n79\t    - strconv\n80\t    - strings\n81\t  import_constraints:\n82\t    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n83\t    - \"Must not import any other pkg/ package.\"\n84\t\n85\tacceptance_criteria:\n86\t  - id: AC1\n87\t    criterion: \"Parse('100K') returns 102400.\"\n88\t    traces:\n89\t      - R1.1\n90\t      - R1.2\n91\t  - id: AC2\n92\t    criterion: \"Parse('5MB') returns 5000000.\"\n93\t    traces:\n94\t      - R1.1\n95\t      - R1.3\n96\t  - id: AC3\n97\t    criterion: \"ParseWithOptions('+50M', ParseOptions{AllowSign: true}) returns 52428800.\"\n98\t    traces:\n99\t      - R1.4\n100\t      - R2.1\n101\t  - id: AC4\n102\t    criterion: \"Parse('invalid') returns an error.\"\n103\t    traces:\n104\t      - R3.2\n105\t  - id: AC5\n106\t    criterion: \"pkg/sizeparse compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n107\t    traces:\n108\t      - R1.1\n109\t      - R1.2\n110\t      - R1.3\n111\t      - R1.4\n112\t      - R2.1\n113\t      - R2.2\n114\t      - R3.1\n115\t      - R3.2\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f674801f-0862-4854-b494-5c4a46b54b47","uuid":"86729c09-5bb2-4167-b804-aa038503b998","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:43:16.113Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5081/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd087-sizeparse.yaml","content":"id: srd087-sizeparse\ntitle: \"pkg/sizeparse: Size String Parser with Unit Suffixes\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Eight cmd/ utilities (head, tail, split, truncate, numfmt, du, sort, od) need to parse\n  size strings with unit suffixes (K, M, G, T for powers of 1024; KB, MB, GB for powers\n  of 1000; b for 512). In run 37, each utility independently implemented suffix parsing\n  with slight variations, totaling ~50 lines per utility. This duplication risks\n  inconsistent suffix handling across utilities.\n\n  pkg/sizeparse provides a single parser that handles all GNU coreutils suffix\n  conventions. Each cmd/ utility imports this package instead of reimplementing suffix\n  multiplication.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Parse size strings with binary (IEC) and decimal (SI) unit suffixes.\n  - G2: Support the full GNU coreutils suffix set including b, K, KiB, KB, M, MiB, MB, G, GiB, GB, T, TiB, TB, P, PiB, PB, E, EiB, EB, Z, ZiB, ZB, Y, YiB, YB.\n  - G3: Support optional sign prefixes (+, -) for relative size adjustments.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Size parsing\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func Parse(s string) (int64, error) that parses a size string\n            consisting of an optional sign (+ or -), a decimal integer, and an optional\n            unit suffix. Returns the size in bytes. Returns an error for invalid input.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: |\n            Must support binary (IEC) suffixes: K or KiB (1024), M or MiB (1024^2),\n            G or GiB (1024^3), T or TiB (1024^4), P or PiB (1024^5), E or EiB (1024^6),\n            Z or ZiB (1024^7), Y or YiB (1024^8). Must also support b (512) for\n            POSIX compatibility.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            Must support decimal (SI) suffixes: KB (1000), MB (1000^2), GB (1000^3),\n            TB (1000^4), PB (1000^5), EB (1000^6), ZB (1000^7), YB (1000^8).\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must preserve the sign in the returned value. \"+100K\" returns +102400, \"-50M\" returns -52428800. No sign prefix means positive.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended parsing options\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\n            where ParseOptions allows configuring behavior:\n\n              type ParseOptions struct {\n                  AllowSign    bool // allow +/- prefix (default false for Parse)\n                  DefaultUnit  int64 // multiplier when no suffix given (default 1)\n              }\n      - R2.2:\n          text: \"ParseWithOptions with DefaultUnit=1024 and input '5' returns 5120 (5 * 1024). This supports utilities like du where the default unit is blocks.\"\n\n  R3:\n    title: Constraints\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: Must return an error when the numeric part overflows int64.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: Must return an error when the suffix is not recognized.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sizeparse does not format sizes for output; use pkg/format.HumanSize for that.\n  - pkg/sizeparse does not handle floating-point sizes.\n\npackage_contract:\n  exports:\n    - name: ParseOptions\n      signature: \"type ParseOptions struct { AllowSign bool; DefaultUnit int64 }\"\n    - name: Parse\n      signature: \"func Parse(s string) (int64, error)\"\n    - name: ParseWithOptions\n      signature: \"func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\"\n  imports:\n    - fmt\n    - strconv\n    - strings\n  import_constraints:\n    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n    - \"Must not import any other pkg/ package.\"\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Parse('100K') returns 102400.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"Parse('5MB') returns 5000000.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.3\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"ParseWithOptions('+50M', ParseOptions{AllowSign: true}) returns 52428800.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Parse('invalid') returns an error.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"pkg/sizeparse compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0152Ttzd5pGBEof9LR87yFVE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full SRD. Let me implement the parsing logic. The task focuses on R1.1-R1.4 (core size string parsing), but I also need to implement R2.1-R2.2 (ParseWithOptions) since Parse delegates to ParseWithOptions per design decision D1.\n\nLet me plan the implementation:\n\n1. ParseOptions struct - already defined, keep as-is\n2. Parse delegates to ParseWithOptions with zero-value options\n3. ParseWithOptions:\n   - Handle optional sign prefix (if AllowSign is true)\n   - Extract numeric part and suffix\n   - Look up the suffix in a mapping table using longest-match-first to get the multiplier\n   - Apply the multiplier to the numeric value and return the result\n\nThe suffix mappings follow a pattern where binary units (KiB, MiB, etc.) use powers of 1024, while decimal units (KB, MB, etc.) use powers of 1000, with the single-letter variants defaulting to binary. The table extends all the way up to yottabytes.\n\nNow I'm noticing a potential conflict in the requirements—R1.1 describes parsing an optional sign, but R2.1 indicates that AllowSign defaults to false for the Parse function. Let me check if R1.4's example of \"+100K\" returning +102400 actually requires sign support by default, or if that's a separate configuration option.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, AC3 specifically uses ParseWithOptions with AllowSign set to true for sign handling, which suggests the basic Parse function probably rejects signs by default. So R1.4 might be demonstrating the behavior when signs are explicitly enabled, not necessarily what the default Parse does.\n\nReviewing the task requirements more carefully, I see that R1-R4 at the task level correspond directly to SRD R1.1-R1.4 under the \"Size parsing\" group. Since both Parse and ParseWithOptions are already stubbed out, I need to implement working versions of both. Parse will delegate to ParseWithOptions, and even though the extended options like AllowSign and DefaultUnit are technically part of R2.1-R2.2, they're necessary for the basic implementation to function properly.\n\nWriting the implementation now... Defining the suffix multiplier table... Defining the binary suffixes...\n\nNow I'm implementing the main parsing functions that handle human-readable size strings with optional sign support and configurable unit defaults, parsing the numeric portion and applying the appropriate multiplier based on the suffix.\n\nContinuing with the string splitting logic... Writing the suffix lookup function...\n\nI'm reconsidering whether suffix matching should be case-sensitive. The SRD specifies exact suffixes like K, KiB, KB with specific casing, and GNU utilities typically treat them case-sensitively for single letters. I'll implement exact case-sensitive matching as the SRD defines them.\n\nFor overflow handling, I need to check if the multiplication of the numeric part and multiplier could exceed int64 limits, since ParseInt only validates the numeric portion itself. Though the task focuses on R1.1-R1.4, returning sensible errors for invalid input is part of the basic requirements. I also need to clarify the sign handling—R1.1 mentions optional signs, but R2.1 says AllowSign defaults to false, so I should respect that configuration. The acceptance criteria are the deciding factor here—AC3 explicitly tests sign handling through ParseWithOptions with AllowSign enabled, while Parse itself has no tests for signs. So I'll stick with the design decision: Parse delegates to ParseWithOptions with default options that disable sign parsing, making sign support opt-in only.\n\nNow I need to think through the overflow handling for the multiplication step. R3.1 covers numeric overflow during the initial parsing phase, which strconv.ParseInt already handles, but the multiplication by the size multiplier is a separate operation that could also overflow—I should decide whether to add an explicit check for that. I'll skip overflow checking on multiplication since it's not in R1.1-R1.4, and R3.1/R3.2 are separate requirements that will presumably be handled in a future task.\n\nLooking at the task requirements more carefully, these are task-level IDs mapping to SRD R1.1-R1.4. To make Parse and ParseWithOptions work, I need to implement the full function including error handling, and the SRD-level constraints about overflow and unrecognized suffixes are natural error paths that R1.1 already implies.\n\nI'll implement the complete parser using the suffix table approach from D2. The table's already ordered for longest-match-first, but since I'm splitting the input cleanly into numeric and suffix parts, I just need exact matching rather than prefix matching. Now I'm figuring out how to split the numeric part from the suffix.\n\nWriting the parser implementation...\n\nNow I need to verify the multiplier values are correct. Let me calculate the powers of 1024 to make sure they fit within int64 limits—1024^6 is around 1.15 × 10^18, which is safely below the int64 maximum of about 9.2 × 10^18, so I have room for binary units up through exabytes.\n\nHowever, 1024^7 and anything larger overflow int64, and the same issue applies to the SI equivalents like 10^21 for zettabytes. Since these larger suffixes would always overflow when multiplied by any reasonable value, I should note that limitation.\n\nThe practical approach is to include the multipliers that actually fit in int64—like E (exabytes at 10^18)—and acknowledge that Z and Y suffixes can't be properly represented as constants. I'll include as many as possible in the suffix table while being aware that parsing something like \"1Z\" will inevitably overflow. that Go won't let me assign an overflowing constant directly. I need to handle Z and Y suffixes specially—either by detecting them upfront and returning an overflow error, or by using a different approach like storing them as strings or using big.Int for the multipliers. The cleanest solution is probably to detect Z/Y early and fail with an overflow error, since any nonzero value multiplied by those suffixes will exceed int64 anyway. overflowing constant to int64. Go constants are arbitrary precision, and assigning `1024 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024` to an int64 variable will cause a compile error: \"constant overflows int64\".\n\nSo I need a different approach for Z and Y. The spec requires supporting these suffixes, which means recognizing them even if the result overflows. The error should come from overflow detection, not from an unrecognized suffix. I think the cleanest solution is to compute the multipliers at runtime for Z and Y rather than as compile-time constants, which avoids the overflow error entirely. Defining suffix multipliers with a map... Completing the suffix map...\n\nI realize using -1 as a sentinel won't work since it's a valid int64 value that could cause issues downstream. I need a different approach to flag overflow cases.\n\nLet me check what actually fits in int64: E and EB can represent up to about 1.15 × 10^18, which is within the int64 limit of ~9.2 × 10^18. But Z, ZB, Y, and YB would overflow since they'd need values around 10^21 or higher. So I'll use a named constant like 0 as a sentinel value to mark when a suffix causes overflow.\n\nActually, using 0 as a sentinel is confusing since map lookups can return 0 for \"not found.\" A cleaner approach is to maintain a separate set of overflow suffixes that I check first before looking up multipliers. This way the logic is explicit and unambiguous.\n\nWriting the final implementation... Completing the suffix mappings and defining overflow handling...\n\nNow I'm implementing the main parsing function that takes a size string and optional configuration, validating the input, splitting it into its components, parsing the numeric part, resolving the unit multiplier, and then applying the sign to get the final result. Still writing parser... B, ..., Z/ZiB, Y/YiB, b) - ✅ (Z/Y return overflow error but are recognized)\nR1.3: SI suffixes (KB, MB, ..., ZB, YB) - ✅ (Z/Y return overflow error but are recognized)\nR1.4: Sign preservation - ✅ (\"+100K\" → +102400, \"-50M\" → -52428800 when AllowSign is true)\n\nDesign decisions:\nD1: Parse delegates to ParseWithOptions with zero-value ParseOptions - ✅\nD2: Suffix matching uses a table, longest-match-first - ✅ (using map + exact match after digit split; no ambiguity since suffix is extracted as a complete string)\nD3: Imports limited to fmt and strconv - ✅\n\nNow I'm checking the edge cases to make sure the implementation handles them correctly: zero with and without suffixes, single-byte values, empty strings, missing digits, and sign handling with the AllowSign flag enabled and disabled.\n\nAll three helper functions stay well under the 40-line limit, and the overall file will be comfortably under 500 lines.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to include requirement IDs in the comments for clarity, but I'll keep the implementation straightforward and let the code speak for itself. I'm reviewing the acceptance criteria to make sure my implementation meets all requirements—the signatures for ParseOptions, Parse, and ParseWithOptions need to stay exactly as specified, and I need to verify that all behaviors from R1.1 through R1.4 are properly implemented before writing the final file.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1747,"cache_read_input_tokens":154894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1747},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f674801f-0862-4854-b494-5c4a46b54b47","uuid":"297ab7d2-080f-4423-a1fd-1e3f35177509"}
